## Ruled intersections

The maintainer ruled three intersections in carve#5658 on 2026-11-09. R03: “A lone `+` at the host's marker column on the line right after a refused brace run stays literal paragraph text.” F06: “the shifted line misses the item” and “opens a document-level fence.” The follow-up ruling says that a fence attached by a marker-line `+` keeps a nonblank line without the host prefix as payload until its first blank. R03 follows CARVE-P2-035's refused-run protection; F06 and the marker-line cells follow the attachment rule at the top of this table.

## Ruling excerpts used by the cells

- carve#5549: “the Expected section stands” and “The rule measures from the `{` itself.”
- carve#5560: “A, keep the blank” for an own-line `+` fence.
- carve#5566: “`- li` continues the paragraph in both hosts.”
- carve#5579: “the shifted closer at column 1 closes the attached fence.”
- carve#5587: “A, the closer column alone decides.”
- carve#5600: “An indented closer is the refused run's follower closer.”
- carve#5601: “B, the note” for the refused-marker follower.
- carve#5606: “A, fold in every owner.”
- carve#5618: “B, an open code span hides the run.”
- carve#5619: “the lazy line supplies its written source column.”
- carve#5635: “A, an item reads it like a note.”
- carve#5636: “the fold applies after every `+` fence that ended at its blank.”
- carve#5637: “A, the item keeps the line.”
- carve#5638: “inside a still-open inline code span, `{.x}` is code text.”
- carve#5639: “the `+` reaches the description or note body.”

## Boundary controls

These cells name hosts whose source columns are measured through quote prefixes, and the root paragraph that cannot take a `+`. Their input and expected HTML fragment are written with `\n` for a source newline. The table test walks these rows too.

